Use this page when one program should be reachable over more than one
transport, and you want its sturdyrefs to keep working when those transports
change.

A standard OCapN locator names one transport and one designator. So a program
that listens on two transports looks like two separate peers, and a sturdyref
written for one of them only works over that one path. glowcap's multi-home
support solves this with the `id` locator. It names the peer by its durable
public key and lists the paths that reach it as hints.

Multi-homing works in glowcap today. Its wire names are not yet part of OCapN,
so they live in glowcap's private trial profile, `glowcap-next`.
[Standing]($link.ref('standing')) at the end explains what that means for you.

# One peer, several routes

An `id` locator puts the peer's durable public key in the designator, the
same place other keyed transports put their names. Its transport symbol is
`id`, but `id` is not a byte path of its own. It is a bundle of routes, each
of which is a real transport.

Dialing an `id` locator tries its routes in order. What makes the answering
program the right peer is never the route it came in on. It is the channel
proving it holds the key the locator names.

[Dialing one multi-home peer: the first route is down, so the dial moves on, and the next route's channel proves the key. That proof is what makes whoever answered the peer. The weak fallback can carry a signed gift, never the identity.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-multihome-dial.svg'))

The routes are written into two hints on a single `ocapn://` URI. Each route
is itself a locator, percent-encoded inside the hint and separated by `|`.
Decoded for reading, the shape is:

```
ocapn://<durable key>.id?via=<route>|<route>&alt-locators=<route>
```

The `via` hint lists the strong routes. A route is strong when its channel can
prove a durable key on its own:

- [iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')) authenticates its node id in the QUIC
  handshake.
- The [Noise paths]($link.page('transports/noise')) prove the dialed key
  while sealing the stream.
- [WebRTC]($link.page('transports/webrtc')) binds the peer through its
  signaling authority, so it is as strong as your trust in that authority.

A cleartext keyed route is not strong, because its key is only an address to
dial and nothing proves it.

The `alt-locators` hint lists weak fallbacks, such as a plain socket or an
[onion]($link.page('transports/onion')) address. Tor proves an onion service
only to the side that dials it, so an onion route cannot prove a peer in both
directions. A weak route can complete
one handoff that a signed gift already authorized, and nothing more. It never
establishes the peer's identity, it never enters the identity cache, and a
sturdyref never dials it.

Each list holds at most eight routes, and the two together are limited to a
few kilobytes. Every `via` route must name the same key as the designator.
These limits mean an untrusted locator cannot cause unbounded work or slip in
a second identity.

# What the other peer sees

Routes are a negotiated feature called `multi_location`. glowcap offers it
under its own `glowcap-next` profile, and both sides must record it in their
signed hellos before either may use it. A first hello never includes it.

The decision is made per session. One process can use routes with another
glowcap host while its session with a
[Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/) vat stays on the standard
profile.

[A negotiated partner receives the id bundle intact. A baseline partner gets one concrete route it can dial, with the private vocabulary stripped, and when no such route exists the give is refused before anything is sent.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-multihome-lowering.svg'))

A handoff can cross between those two kinds of session, because a signed give
travels from the exporter's session to the recipient's. The give keeps its
routes only when both sessions negotiated `multi_location`. Otherwise glowcap
gives the recipient one concrete route on a transport that recipient is known
to use, with the private fields removed. If no such route exists, glowcap
refuses the give before sending anything.

glowcap never sends experimental fields in the hope that the other side will
ignore them. That is why the wire a Goblins peer sees is the same whether or
not multi-homing is in use.

# Sturdyrefs that survive a transport change

This is where multi-homing pays off. A sturdyref written against an `id`
locator names the peer's key, not one of its paths. The peer can move to a
different transport, or add a second listener, and stored references keep
working.

Redeeming such a sturdyref works like this, on a Host that has opted into
`multi_location`:

1. If a session already exists whose channel proved that key, on any
   transport, the Host reuses it.
2. Otherwise it tries the strong `via` routes, two at a time, in the order the
   publisher listed them. When one attempt fails, it starts the next route on
   the list. The first channel that proves the key carries the fetch, and the
   other dials are cancelled.
3. Only after every strong route fails does it try the weak fallbacks, one at
   a time, in their listed order.

The
[multi-home route diagrams](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/08-multihome-routes.md)
draw each of these flows.

# Rooms uses it

Directory peering in the [Rooms app]($link.page('examples')) uses multi-homing
live. A Rooms server started with `--peering` can list one of its durable
rooms in another server's directory under a single `id` locator. Its iroh and
Noise listeners, which prove the key, go under `via`. Its onion and plain
socket listeners go under `alt-locators` as weak fallbacks.

Each reader then joins the room over whichever path that reader can open, so
the listing does not pick one transport for everyone. The listing belongs to
the publisher's session with that directory. Revoking or disconnecting it
removes the room from the directory, but a room already handed to a reader
stays live.

# [Standing]($section.id('standing'))

The OCapN community has wanted multi-homing for years but has not specified
it yet. Its meeting notes discuss ideas ranging from locators that name
several netlayers to a vat id that does not depend on any transport. glowcap
builds it now because it already has the pieces: keyed transports over
several kinds of byte path, and a sealed channel that proves a durable key
over any of them.

Everything described above is built and covered by the build checks. That
includes the route bundle, the ordered dial with its bounded race and identity
rules, the per-session negotiation and fallback, `id` sturdyrefs, and the
Rooms peering suite that exercises them live. The decision record behind this
design is
[ADR-0125](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0125-identity-route-multihome.md).

What remains open is the wire format. It stays a `glowcap-next` extension
until OCapN specifies a compatible one. Peers that never agree on the
extension never see these route hints, and a Goblins peer keeps getting
ordinary locators. [What you can depend on]($link.page('stability')) covers
the evidence behind claims like these.
